this is a purple tomato just kidding  it's your typical garden variety type  but the purple ones do exist tomatoes  that are purple all the way through  aren't available anywhere in the world  just yet that's because they're  genetically modified but they're on the  cusp of getting the approval from  regulators to end up on your plate and  the journey of this little tomato from a  lab in the uk to your salad bowl could  be a really big deal to start in lab  experiments cans of prone mice that were  fed purple tomato powder live 30 longer  but nutrition is only one part of the  story it's also about the shelf life of  the food we eat and its origins let's  dig in a gmo or genetically modified  organism is an organism whose genetic  material has been altered using genetic  engineering to include the genes of  another organism believe it or not this  could be the second genetically modified  tomato to hit shelves the flavor saver  tomato was sold in grocery stores in the  90s but at the end of the day it was  kind of a flop because it was expensive  to produce and market plus there was  public distrust and backlash against  gmos a distress that kind of persists to  this day are they safe  are they natural who knows  we'll get to that but first let's talk  about nutrition the purple gmo tomatoes  that could go on sale sometime soon are  purple all the way through because  they're loaded with antioxidants called  anthocyanins they're found in foods like  blueberries and blackberries and they  give those fruits their signature colors  nutrition experts in studies say that  these molecules are good for us because  they help ourselves fight off their  version of stress which helps them stay  healthy longer nearly 20 years ago kathy  martin who's a plant geneticist at the  john innes center in the uk set out to  make a tomato that was loaded with  anthocyanins we thought that this was a  cool way of doing something that was  quite attractive and a bit different to  try to make a better tomato she and her  team inserted two jeans from the  snapdragon into regular tomatoes those  jeans act like a switch to turn on the  production of anthocyanins in a study  from 2008 kathy and her team showed that  cancer-prone mice whose diet was  supplemented with purple tomato powder  live longer than those supplemented with  red tomato powder and if you think that  that's just a change in diet no drugs  just a change in diet that's pretty  phenomenal really other food science  experts i spoke to warned that the  effects we see in lab animals don't  always pan out in humans but everyone  seemed to agree that fruits and veggies  should be the foundation of a good diet  another benefit of these gmo purple  tomatoes is their longer shelf life  that's good because it could make them  more commercially viable and reduce food  waste they have double the shelf life so  this tomato has been sitting on the  bench for about about six weeks her  purple tomato doesn't wrinkle or go soft  as fast as a regular tomato plus it's  also less susceptible to gray mold but  there are two big questions around gmos  are they natural and are they safe at  first glance the natural question seems  to have a pretty obvious answer  no because gmos don't exist in nature  but my conversation with harry klee a  plant biologist at the university of  florida made me think that maybe it's  not quite that simple he told me that  many of the foods we eat today aren't  all natural either they're the product  of years of deliberate genetic tweaking  after world war ii the international  atomic energy agency used to routinely  take batches of seeds like wheat corn  and expose them to high levels of  radiation in order to induce mutations  the word mutation kind of makes us  cringe but mutations are actually a  foundational part of nature farmers and  the ag industry leverage mutations or  genetic changes to make their products  better for example the mass production  of seedless grapes and watermelons  didn't happen naturally they are the  product of years of research and  development to make them easier for us  to eat usually to land on a beneficial  mutation developers have to screen  thousands of genetic changes to find the  one they're looking for geneticists say  that gmos and genetic engineering are  just a high-tech faster and more precise  version of that process that brings us  to the question of safety could this  high-speed evolution actually make our  foods unsafe well that's where the  government comes in for a gmo food to  make it to market developers have to get  regulatory approval that involves giving  regulators data on its chemistry and  other characteristics kathy and her  collaborators have done that and they're  waiting to get clearance from regulators  to sell their tomato in the u.s if this  tomato gets approved it would be the  first genetically modified tomato to be  sold directly to consumers since the  1990s  to start the purple tomatoes will be  sold to home growers as seeds what do  they taste like they taste like tomatoes  i can't claim anything special it just  tastes like tomatoes  the researchers i spoke to said that  eating gmos really just boils down to  personal choice they do expect that in  the future more of our food will be  genetically altered through genetic  engineering and recently the fda cleared  the way for the consumption of  gene-edited cattle that are supposed to  be more resistant to extreme heat  gene-edited foods will not be labeled as  gmos because they don't contain genetic  material from another organism so they  don't fit the technical definition of  what a gmo is plus in january regulators  retired the gmo label which will be  replaced by bioengineered or derived  from bioengineering to be perfectly  honest with you i am kind of curious  about what a purple pasta tomato sauce  would taste like or  maybe some purple ketchup to go with my  fries would you try a gmo tomato let us  know in the comments and if you like  this video hit subscribe  thanks for watching i went
 

